{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.ChainBondOrderDependence"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.weighted_diff_supported_on_multidim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.weighted_diff_supported_on_multidim","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L128-L155","doc":"**The cross-arc reconciliation** (`qou-99qc` / #7295), in Lean.\n\nA sibling arc proves the alternating character is order-blind: a 1-dimensional\nrepresentation sends a word to a product of scalars, so it sees only the\nmultiset of letter shifts.  This probe measured a Markov-trace ratio that IS\norder-dependent.  The two are consistent, and this says exactly why: whenever\nevery 1-dimensional block agrees on the two words, the whole difference of a\n`y`-weighted character sum is carried by the blocks of dimension `≠ 1`.\n\nMeasured companion (`H₆`, `G³`, 60 dps): the 1-dimensional blocks `(6)` and\n`(1⁶)` contribute `9.0e-62` in total — the arithmetic floor — against `1.729e-3`\nfrom the rest. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.bondOf_ne_secondOnly_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.bondOf_ne_secondOnly_witness","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L118-L122","doc":"Anti-vacuity the other way: an explicit non-constant `f` over `Bool`, so the\nexcluded case is inhabited too. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_const_is_bondOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_const_is_bondOf","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L112-L116","doc":"Anti-vacuity: the constant case really is inside the family, so the iff does\nnot hold merely because its left side is always false. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.not_exists_bondOf_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.not_exists_bondOf_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L106-L110","doc":"The contrapositive — the form the measurement lands in.  `μ(p,p) ≠ μ(n,n)`\nwas measured, so the γγ bond factor is not any `bondOf`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_eq_bondOf_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_eq_bondOf_iff","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L90-L104","doc":"**The exclusion.** A second-argument-only bond lies in the `bondOf` family\nonly when it is constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_comm_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly_comm_iff","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L80-L88","doc":"A second-argument-only bond is symmetric exactly when it is constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.bondOf_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.bondOf_comm","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L74-L78","doc":"`bondOf` is symmetric in its two nucleon arguments, by construction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.secondOnly","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/SecondArgBondNotPairFunction.lean#L70-L72","doc":"A bond scalar that reads only the **second** nucleon of the ordered pair.\nThis is the shape the γγ bond factor was measured to have. ","signatureChunk":0}]}